<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gjon Kastrioti</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1437">1437</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as John Castriot</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gjon Kastrioti was an Albanian lord who built a principality in the Mat valley and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diber">Dibër</a> region, reaching towards the Adriatic near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lezhe">Lezhë</a>. He accepted Ottoman overlordship while trading with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and Ragusa, and sent his sons as hostages to the Ottoman court. One of them was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skanderbeg">Gjergj Kastrioti</a>, known as Skanderbeg.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gjon-kastrioti.jpg" alt="Gjon Kastrioti" width="192" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GjonKastrioti.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The Kastrioti family came from a region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> between Mat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diber">Dibër</a> and Has, and used the name Mazreku, horse breeder, to mark their tribal affiliation. Gjon's father is called Georgius Castriotus, lord of Aemathia and Castoria, by Giovanni Andrea Angelo Flavio Comneno, and Paulo Castrioto by Gjon Muzaka, who says he ruled no more than two villages. That claim is disputed, since noble families built their influence over several generations. The Kastrioti first appear in archival records in Gjon's time, at the end of the 14th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Gjon commanded some 2,000 horsemen and governed the land between the Cape of Rodon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diber">Dibër</a>. In 1402 he led his retinue for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Sultan Bayezid I</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ankara">Battle of Ankara</a>, and in the vacuum after the Ottoman defeat he strengthened his position towards the coast. He gave merchants safe passage, made no claim on Venetian territory and became a citizen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> with his heirs in 1413. Customs from Ragusan traders, grain exports and salt were his main income; in 1424 Venice forced him to destroy the salt pans he had built to escape its monopoly.</p><p>From 1419 to 1426 he was an ally of the Serbian Despot Stefan Lazarević, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a> led by one of his sons joined the despot's war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a>, though in 1423 he left Lazarević's ranks and refused Venetian money to fight him. After an Ottoman vassalage begun between 1415 and 1417, Venice encouraged him to rebel in 1428. In April 1430 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> under Isak-Beg, sanjakbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a>, took most of his land, leaving him only a small part, and he was defeated again after joining the uprising of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gjergj-arianiti">Gjergj Arianiti</a> of 1432–36.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Gjon is said to have changed faith with his alliances: Catholic as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>'s ally, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> as Lazarević's, Muslim from 1431, and a saying of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>, where the sword is, there lies religion, is believed to come from him. In 1426 he gave the taxes of two villages to the Serbian monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hilandar">Hilandar</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a>, where his son Reposh died in 1431, and its Saint George Tower became known as the Albanian Tower. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> listed his former territory as Yuvan-ili, from the Turkish form of his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gjon Kastrioti” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Gjon Kastrioti</b> built a lordship stretching from the Mat valley towards the Adriatic coast near Lezhë, accepting Ottoman overlordship after the 1380s while trading with Venice and Ragusa.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-kastrioti">Principality of Kastrioti</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mat District, in what is now <a href="/countries/albania">Albania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.57,20.02&amp;year=1427" aria-label="Mat District on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5038 682 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Gjon Kastrioti"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5488 952h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5488 952h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.57,20.02&amp;year=1427">What was here in 1427? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
